MY IDEAS (challenge them)
1. Filter order: requestId/ThreadContext filter -> AMToken auth filter -> region authorization filter (x-region must be in the user's allowed regions) -> set RegionContext -> controller. Clear everything in finally.
2. STATELESS security. No user/region state in statics, singletons or shared contexts.
3. AuthTokenResolver interface with two implementations: JWT (Nimbus JOSE or spring-security-oauth2-resource-server; check signature, exp, iss, aud) and opaque (validate against the AM/LDAP endpoint). The result is an immutable AuthenticatedUser(psid, role, allowedRegions, globalAccess), which becomes the Spring Authentication.
4. Short-TTL Caffeine cache keyed by token hash (TTL <= token expiry), with timeouts and circuit breaker on the LDAP call. Fail closed.
5. @PreAuthorize + custom RegionAccessEvaluator. Maker-checker segregation (checker cannot approve own maker record). Role comes from the token, never from the client.
6. Propagate SecurityContext + RegionContext + ThreadContext into per-region pools via TaskDecorator / DelegatingSecurityContextExecutor, and always clear after. Bulkhead per region.
7. Region connect keeps only the DB/HZ health check. The access decision comes from token-derived data.
8. Logging: ThreadContext keys psid, region, requestId. Return X-Request-Id. Never log cookie/token. Separate audit log for auth success/failure/denied.
9. Consistent JSON errors: 401 vs 403 vs 503 via AuthenticationEntryPoint / AccessDeniedHandler.

